<Base title="About" description="What Readback is, how it works, and how to reach us.">
  <h1>About Readback</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    In aviation, a <em>readback</em> is repeating a transmission back to prove you received
    it correctly. This site reads back to the public what the state collects about them:
    where the licence plate readers are, what the hardware and software can actually do,
    what rules govern them — and what the documents say when we ask.
  </p>

  <h2>The method: facts only</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Every claim on this site is backed by a primary source — certification filings,
    vendor datasheets and marketing, statutes, tribunal decisions, audits, peer-reviewed
    studies, or on-the-record reporting. Where we analyze rather than report, the analysis
    is explicitly marked, and it extends only capabilities the vendors document themselves.
    Where something is unverified, we either say so or don't publish it. The entire
    research base — every source behind every page — is
    <a href="https://github.com/mikeshoss/Readback" rel="noopener">open on GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Readback is not anti-police, and it does not claim anyone is breaking the rules.
    Its core observation is narrower and better documented: on these systems, nearly every
    safeguard is a configuration setting — retention, sharing, permissions — and no one
    outside each police service checks what the settings are. Toll cameras and border
    readers are mapped but shown separately, because a toll you pay and a border you cross
    are not the same as a dragnet on your commute.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect about you</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    We ask police services what they collect and how long they keep it, so we answer the same
    question about ourselves to the same standard — every party that sees something when you
    read this site, what each collects, how long they keep it, and the documentation that says
    so, including the places where no one documents it.
    <a href="/tracking">What we track, in full →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Data &amp; licensing</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Camera locations come from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors,
    <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/" rel="noopener">ODbL</a>) and are
    re-published under the same licence. Fleet, policy, and hardware data are compiled
    from the public record with citations throughout. Corrections: if anything on this
    site is wrong, tell us — we fix fast and log the change publicly in the repository
    history.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs this</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Readback is built and maintained by Mike Shoss, drawing on professional experience
    building AI systems — which is the lens behind the marked analysis sections. It is
    independent: no funding from, and no affiliation with, any vendor, police service,
    or political organization.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    <a href="mailto:themiltonrecord@gmail.com">themiltonrecord@gmail.com</a> — tips, corrections, camera
    reports, FOI results from your region, and media inquiries all welcome. To add a
    camera to the map, the best route is tagging it in
    <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap</a>
    (<code>man_made=surveillance</code> + <code>surveillance:type=ALPR</code>) — that way
    every project built on the shared data benefits, not just this one.
  </p>
</Base>
